# Typeface

Set all Civica text in Roboto, at 2 weights: regular and bold.

Roboto is the typeface Home Assistant already uses on its own screens, so a dashboard needs no extra download. It is free to use and share under the SIL Open Font Licence.

## Font stack

Use this font stack in a custom card or a theme:

```css
font-family: Roboto, "Helvetica Neue", Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
```

If Roboto is missing, the device uses Helvetica Neue, then Helvetica, then Arial, then its own sans-serif font. Do not add another typeface to the stack, and do not download or bundle a font with a card. The Civica wordmark is artwork, not a font. Do not use it to set text.

The Civica theme sets the Home Assistant font variables for you, including `ha-font-family-body` and `ha-font-family-heading`. Code editors keep a monospace font, `'Courier New', monospace`, because the alignment of code carries meaning.

<h2 id="bg-typ-01">Use Roboto everywhere Civica sets the text</h2>

Use Roboto on dashboards, in custom cards, in documentation, on printed handovers and in social images. Screens the phone draws itself keep the phone's own font. [Web-app typography](typography-web-app.html#the-phones-own-screens) explains which screens those are.

## Weights

| Weight | Value | Use it for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Regular | 400 | Body text, labels, hints, readings and buttons |
| Bold | 700 | Headings, and a few words that need emphasis |

Do not use a medium weight in a card or theme. Home Assistant does not provide one, so devices show regular instead and the difference you designed disappears. The Civica theme sets Home Assistant's medium weight, `ha-font-weight-medium`, to 400 for this reason.

A light weight (300) is for the lead paragraph on a documentation page or a custom panel that loads Roboto itself. Do not use it in a card on a dashboard.

